        <p>Preventing a <strong>lack of sleep</strong> isn't just about forcing yourself to lie in bed earlier. It requires building healthy, sustainable habits that signal your brain it is safe and time to wind down. By prioritizing sleep hygiene, you can stop sleep debt before it wreaks havoc on your health.</p>
        
        <p>If you want to protect your energy, focus, and long-term health, apply these key prevention strategies tonight.</p>

        <h2>1. Fix Your Wake-Up Schedule (Even on Weekends)</h2>
        <p>Your body runs on a natural 24-hour biological clock known as the circadian rhythm. When you sleep in late on Saturdays and Sundays, you create "social jetlag," which destroys your sleep cycle for Monday. Set an alarm for the exact same time every single morning to anchor your internal clock.</p>

        <h2>2. Establish a 30-Minute Digital Sunset</h2>
        <p>The screens on your smartphone, laptop, and television emit a powerful concentrated frequency of <strong>blue light</strong>. This specific light tricks your brain into thinking it is still daytime, instantly suppressing the production of <strong>melatonin</strong> (the sleep hormone). Turn off all screens 30 minutes before bed and read a physical book instead.</p>

