        <h2>3. Optimize Your Bedroom Environment</h2>
        <p>Your brain naturally drops its core temperature to initiate deep sleep stages. To assist this natural process, ensure your sleep space meets the following conditions:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Keep your room cool (ideally around 65°F to 68°F or 18°C to 20°C).</li>
            <li><strong>Light:</strong> Use premium blackout curtains or a comfortable eye mask to eliminate streetlights.</li>
            <li><strong>Noise:</strong> Use a white noise machine or earplugs to block disruptive neighborhood sounds.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>4. Implement a Caffeine Cut-Off Time</h2>
        <p>Caffeine is a powerful stimulant that blocks adenosine receptors—the chemicals in your brain that build up throughout the day to make you feel sleepy. Because caffeine has a half-life of up to 6 to 8 hours, that afternoon coffee or energy drink is likely still circulating in your blood at midnight. Stop consuming caffeine by 2:00 PM.</p>

        <h2>5. Don’t Lie Awake in Bed</h2>
        <p>If you cannot fall asleep after 20 minutes, get out of bed. Remaining in bed while feeling anxious teaches your brain a destructive psychological association: the bed means frustration, not sleep. Move to a dimly lit room, do a quiet activity like stretching or journaling, and return to bed only when your eyelids feel genuinely heavy.</p>
